WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.520
What's up? Welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners, Episode 109 for July 21st, 2026.

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:11.420
Let's break down today's coolest AI news so anyone can understand it.

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:17.020
Today's AI news is pretty exciting, and I promise no jargon. Let's go.

00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:20.840
Here's something that's going to change how you think about making movies.

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:28.880
A director who created the film District 9 just released a brand new 13-minute sci-fi horror short called Nightborn.

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:36.720
Every single frame came from an AI video model named Seedance 2.0 instead of cameras or actors.

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:45.920
The director typed detailed text descriptions for each scene and guided the process step-by-step until the pieces formed a complete story.

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:53.920
Think of it like describing a vivid dream to someone who can instantly turn those words into moving pictures that play like a real film.

00:00:53.920 --> 00:01:00.660
He kept adjusting the descriptions the same way a traditional director might reshoot a scene until it felt right.

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:06.560
This matters because short films usually need crews, locations, and big budgets.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:11.800
Yet one person with a laptop created something that used to require an entire studio.

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:19.940
For anyone who makes TikTok videos, YouTube sketches, or school projects, it shows a new creative path is opening up fast.

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:26.660
You can search for Nightborn Neil Blomkamp on YouTube right now and watch the whole thing yourself.

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:33.220
Pay attention to which parts already feel like a polished movie, and which parts still look a little strange,

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:36.960
because that gap shows exactly where the technology is heading next.

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:44.040
Okay, now for my favorite part of the show, the deep dive where we figure out how the technology actually works.

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:48.940
You know how your phone sometimes predicts the next word while you are typing a message?

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:56.460
Today's AI video tools do something similar, except they predict the next tiny slice of a moving picture instead of a word.

00:01:57.380 --> 00:02:06.120
First, the system studies millions of short video clips to learn everyday patterns like how arms swing during a walk or how shadows slide across a floor.

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:13.040
When you type a description, the model creates one frame and then guesses what the following frame should look like.

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:21.240
It repeats that guess over and over, building motion the same way you flip through a stack of drawings to create a flip book animation.

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:29.900
Because it has seen so many examples, it can add realistic details you never mentioned, such as the way fabric folds when someone turns.

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:37.500
The hard part is keeping every frame consistent so a character's appearance does not suddenly change halfway through the scene.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.260
That steady frame by frame prediction is what turns a written description into footage you can actually watch as a short film.

00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:55.640
Once you see the process this way, the whole idea stops feeling mysterious and starts feeling like a very fast, very large flip book that learned from real life.

00:02:56.180 --> 00:02:59.540
Let's shift to something you can try on your own phone today.

00:02:59.540 --> 00:03:07.820
Adobe released an experimental camera app called Project Indigo that gives iPhone photos a more natural film-like look.

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:16.440
The latest version added generative AI tools that can remove unwanted objects from a shot while you are still looking through the viewfinder.

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:22.860
It also offers instant suggestions on framing and composition before you even take the picture.

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:30.960
The tools run live inside the camera screen so you see the changes happen in real time instead of editing later on a computer.

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:36.760
If you have an iPhone, open the app store and search for Project Indigo by Adobe.

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:44.580
Once it is installed, point the camera at something ordinary like your desk or window and test the Remove Distractions option.

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:52.520
Watch how the app cleans up the scene on the spot and notice whether the result still feels natural or looks a little too perfect.

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:59.700
That quick experiment shows you exactly how these tools are moving from post-production into the moment you press the shutter.

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:03.920
Before we wrap up, here are a couple of quick bits worth knowing.

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:15.040
A team at Xiaomi trained a robot called Xiaomi Robotics 1 on more than 100,000 hours of people using simple handheld grippers with cameras attached.

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:24.280
Surprisingly, giving the robot more real-world movement examples improved its skills more than simply making the underlying AI model larger.

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:36.220
The gains kept appearing even after the model had already grown quite big, which suggests that for physical tasks real-movement data can matter more than raw computing power right now.

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:42.340
That's it for today. Remember, every AI expert started exactly where you are right now.

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:48.600
If something we talked about today made you curious, go try it. That's literally how learning works.

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:52.360
Stay curious, keep experimenting, and we'll see you tomorrow.

